        </example>
        <example title="Release Type Guidelines">
          <correct-example title="Proper release type classification" conditions="Determining release increment type" expected-result="Correctly classified release type" correctness-criteria="Follows semantic versioning rules for major/minor/patch classification"><![CDATA[// MAJOR Release (x.0.0) - Breaking changes:
- API changes requiring user action
- Database schema changes affecting compatibility  
- New major features changing core functionality
Example: 2.5.3 → 3.0.0

// MINOR Release (x.x.0) - New features, backward compatible:
- New dashboard functionality
- New search features
- Enhanced PWA capabilities  
Example: 2.0.0 → 2.1.0

// PATCH Release (x.x.x) - Bug fixes and patches:
- UI styling fixes
- TypeScript compilation errors
- Performance improvements
- Documentation updates
Example: 2.1.0 → 2.1.1]]></correct-example>
          <incorrect-example title="Incorrect release type classification" conditions="Determining release increment type" expected-result="Correctly classified release type" incorrectness-criteria="Wrong semantic versioning application or skipping user confirmation"><![CDATA[// WRONG - Major release for minor changes:
AI: "This UI fix will be a major release" // Should be patch

// WRONG - Automatic decision without user input:
// AI decides release type without asking user preference

// WRONG - Inconsistent incrementing:
2.1.0 → 2.3.0  // Skipped 2.2.0 without major breaking change]]></incorrect-example>
        </example>
